WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.334 align:start size:94% position:6%
-New York is a character
in this show.

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.702 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes, as it often is.
-As it often is.

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:05.839
You were the first one to think,
"This was a character."

00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:09.142 align:start size:94% position:6%
But you put it
in your neighborhood.

00:00:09.142 --> 00:00:10.744 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's an East Village show.
-Yes, and I love it.

00:00:10.744 --> 00:00:13.113
-And now you're walking around,
you basically now are --

00:00:13.113 --> 00:00:15.548 size:81% position:41%
-you are very much the
character you played,

00:00:15.548 --> 00:00:16.750 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you're walking
around the set.

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:19.819 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It has become some sort of a
meta-snow-globe nightmare

00:00:19.819 --> 00:00:21.855 align:start size:94% position:6%
like I cannot even describe.
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:21.855 --> 00:00:25.525
-The other day, I walked out,
and it's like, in my mind,

00:00:25.525 --> 00:00:27.694 size:78% position:39%
it's going to be like
Christopher Walken

00:00:27.694 --> 00:00:29.062 align:start size:94% position:6%
in Abel Ferrara's
"King of New York."

00:00:29.062 --> 00:00:30.930 size:84% position:42%
You know what I mean?
Looking down, coked up,

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:32.799 align:start size:94% position:6%
and what a city, what a city.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:32.799 --> 00:00:35.335 align:start size:94% position:6%
And instead it's me
in the East Village.

00:00:35.335 --> 00:00:39.105
And I kind of -- I was going to
meet a friend for breakfast,

00:00:39.105 --> 00:00:40.373 line:92%
and I was like, "At Odessa,

00:00:40.373 --> 00:00:42.142 align:start size:94% position:6%
across the street
from Tompkins Square Park,"

00:00:42.142 --> 00:00:44.744
which was already ironic.
If you've seen the show --

00:00:44.744 --> 00:00:46.146 align:start size:94% position:6%
spoiler alert --
the diner's in it.

00:00:46.146 --> 00:00:49.182 size:78% position:39%
-[ Chuckling ] Yeah.
-And so, there I was.

00:00:49.182 --> 00:00:50.784 align:start size:94% position:6%
I had sort of forgotten
my iPhone,

00:00:50.784 --> 00:00:53.353 align:start size:94% position:6%
so I was walking around
with, like, an iPad

00:00:53.353 --> 00:00:55.889
as though it was a phone,
which is already like --

00:00:55.889 --> 00:00:58.658 align:start size:94% position:6%
just doesn't make you look
particularly sane,

00:00:58.658 --> 00:01:00.693 align:start size:94% position:6%
if that's the vibe
you're going for.

00:01:00.693 --> 00:01:02.662 line:92%
And I had a big overcoat.

00:01:02.662 --> 00:01:04.230 align:start size:94% position:6%
But it was kind of too sunny,
because who knows

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:06.566 align:start size:94% position:6%
what's happening with
the global warming, et cetera.

00:01:06.566 --> 00:01:09.335 align:start size:94% position:6%
And you never know what season
it's going to be, do you?

00:01:09.335 --> 00:01:13.540
And then I just had a little bit
of ash on my collar.

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:17.710 align:start size:94% position:6%
Don't smoke.
And I stumbled in to Tomkins,

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:21.281 align:start size:94% position:6%
right where I had, like,
shoplifted at Odessa.

00:01:21.281 --> 00:01:22.816 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then I sort of --
there were people there,

00:01:22.816 --> 00:01:26.619
and I ended up in the booth,
directly -- exactly where Nadia,

00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:29.756 align:start size:94% position:6%
the character from the show,
sat waiting for Alan,

00:01:29.756 --> 00:01:30.990 line:92%
the wonderful Charlie Barnett.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:33.193 align:start size:94% position:6%
And instead, I was waiting
for my friend,

00:01:33.193 --> 00:01:34.727
who was not him, thank goodness.
-Yeah.

00:01:34.727 --> 00:01:37.864 size:81% position:41%
-And I realized, like,
"Oh, this is really --

00:01:37.864 --> 00:01:39.833 align:start size:94% position:6%
I've created
a bit of a nightmare,

00:01:39.833 --> 00:01:42.001 align:start size:94% position:6%
where I'm living
within the thing."

00:01:42.001 --> 00:01:45.972
And you know, when I get hit by
a taxi, I won't be surprised,

00:01:45.972 --> 00:01:47.974 align:start size:94% position:6%
suffice to say.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:47.974 --> 00:01:50.310 align:start size:94% position:6%
-One of the things
about your character --

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:51.377 align:start size:94% position:6%
you mentioned
getting hit by a taxi.

00:01:51.377 --> 00:01:55.281
That's one of the many ways your
character dies in New York City.

00:01:55.281 --> 00:01:58.151 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Thank you.
-You strike me as someone --

00:01:58.151 --> 00:02:00.653 align:start size:94% position:6%
do you see danger
everywhere in the city?

00:02:00.653 --> 00:02:03.123 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Sure, you know,
and the place...

00:02:03.123 --> 00:02:04.791 size:88% position:44% line:92%
Danger and yet a relief.

00:02:04.791 --> 00:02:07.227
A sort of a white-noise silence
that would follow.

00:02:07.227 --> 00:02:10.897 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know...
-Because you got to watch --

00:02:10.897 --> 00:02:13.466 align:start size:94% position:6%
Here's a couple
of your body doubles.

00:02:13.466 --> 00:02:16.169 align:start size:94% position:6%
-That's Tawny,
my wonderful stand-in.

00:02:16.169 --> 00:02:17.904 align:start size:94% position:6%
And this is Becca,
who has, I guess,

00:02:17.904 --> 00:02:20.640 align:start size:94% position:6%
soft bones and a rubber body.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.010 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's really --
She is a magical creature.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:25.445 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't know.
But, yes.

00:02:25.445 --> 00:02:29.215
And for me, it was such a joy to
see this many doppelgangers.

00:02:29.215 --> 00:02:31.251 align:start size:94% position:6%
It really speaks to
my sort of brain damage.

00:02:31.251 --> 00:02:34.988 align:start size:94% position:6%
-What spoke to me,
watching that New York thing

00:02:34.988 --> 00:02:40.026
of just the metal door that
leads to the basement stairs --

00:02:40.026 --> 00:02:40.660 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-You're a father.

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:42.729 align:start size:94% position:6%
You can't be walking around
willy-nilly.

00:02:42.729 --> 00:02:44.497 size:88% position:44% line:92%
-And now more than ever,

00:02:44.497 --> 00:02:46.065 align:start size:94% position:6%
because people are on
their phones all the time.

00:02:46.065 --> 00:02:48.434 align:start size:94% position:6%
I can't believe, every day,
the front page

00:02:48.434 --> 00:02:50.370
of "The New York Times" has
another person fell down.

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:51.671 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Maybe it is, and
we're just ignoring it.

00:02:51.671 --> 00:02:54.274
We don't pay attention anymore.
-I'm looking at my phone.

00:02:54.274 --> 00:02:55.475 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm not reading
"The New York Times."

00:02:55.475 --> 00:02:58.478 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, I mean, and definitely,
I think, you know,

00:02:58.478 --> 00:03:01.514
the most problematic, and
I've been intensely vocal

00:03:01.514 --> 00:03:02.982 align:start size:94% position:6%
in a way that
no one listens to --

00:03:02.982 --> 00:03:06.119 align:start size:94% position:6%
is the bicycles, Seth.
The bicycles.

00:03:06.119 --> 00:03:08.221
-You're no fan of the bicycles.
-Just listen,

00:03:08.221 --> 00:03:09.489 align:start size:94% position:6%
who loves the environment
more than me?

00:03:09.489 --> 00:03:15.128 align:start size:94% position:6%
And yet, this bicycle lane is
a nightmare for the city.

00:03:15.128 --> 00:03:16.696 align:start size:94% position:6%
Remember jaywalking?
-Yeah.

00:03:16.696 --> 00:03:19.265
-Jaywalking was the best.
It was like, "I'm a New Yorker."

00:03:19.265 --> 00:03:20.466 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
Right?

00:03:20.466 --> 00:03:21.968 align:start size:94% position:6%
Look at this.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:21.968 --> 00:03:24.671 align:start size:94% position:6%
Jaywalking. And littering!
Littering!

00:03:24.671 --> 00:03:26.940 align:start size:94% position:6%
No, no, littering is bad.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:29.509 align:start size:94% position:6%
But jaywalking was, like,
a real New York joy,

00:03:29.509 --> 00:03:32.278
because you looked kind of cool.
You looked over your shoulder,

00:03:32.278 --> 00:03:33.413 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know what I mean?
-Yeah.

00:03:33.413 --> 00:03:35.148 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You felt like Lou Reed.
And now, instead,

00:03:35.148 --> 00:03:38.685 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's like a bicycle zipping by
in all directions.

00:03:38.685 --> 00:03:40.787 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And, again, I'm a fan
of bikes, as well.

00:03:40.787 --> 00:03:42.288 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't want to be
negative-bikes.

00:03:42.288 --> 00:03:45.325 align:start size:94% position:6%
-No, I love -- like I said
very clearly,

00:03:45.325 --> 00:03:47.927 size:84% position:42%
I love the environment.
-Yeah. We're pro-bikes.

00:03:47.927 --> 00:03:49.429 line:92%
My issue is when bikes think

00:03:49.429 --> 00:03:52.031
that they don't have to
live by the traffic laws.

00:03:52.031 --> 00:03:54.033 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, but only in New York
are you, like,

00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:56.569 size:88% position:44%
"If you're a pedestrian,
I have the right-of-way.

00:03:56.569 --> 00:03:59.806
I'm a taxi driver, I'm Mr. Uber,
I'm in charge.

00:03:59.806 --> 00:04:01.808 align:start size:94% position:6%
Bicycles gives me
the right-of-way."

00:04:01.808 --> 00:04:03.810 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's a New Yorker.
New York spirit.

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:06.012 align:start size:94% position:6%
"I'm always right,
and you're wrong."

00:04:06.012 --> 00:04:07.080 align:start size:94% position:6%
-[ Chuckling ] Yeah.
-Right?

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:09.749
-Well, I think everybody,
you know, just assumes

00:04:09.749 --> 00:04:12.752 align:start size:94% position:6%
that whatever is one step up
is a monster.

00:04:12.752 --> 00:04:14.921 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, if you're a pedestrian,
a bike --

00:04:14.921 --> 00:04:15.989 align:start size:94% position:6%
the fact that somebody
is on two wheels,

00:04:15.989 --> 00:04:19.125 size:81% position:41%
they might as well be,
like, a Hummer S.U.V.

00:04:19.125 --> 00:04:20.093 line:92%
You're like, "You monster!"

00:04:20.093 --> 00:04:21.928 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Survival of the fittest --
this city.

00:04:21.928 --> 00:04:24.831
-One of the things that
has already been asked --

00:04:24.831 --> 00:04:25.465 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
and I should note

00:04:25.465 --> 00:04:28.468 align:start size:94% position:6%
that if you never made another
episode of "Russian Doll,"

00:04:28.468 --> 00:04:29.969 align:start size:94% position:6%
you would be
in a great situation

00:04:29.969 --> 00:04:32.205 align:start size:94% position:6%
of having created
this perfectly formed thing.

00:04:32.205 --> 00:04:35.375
But people are asking you a lot,
I would assume,

00:04:35.375 --> 00:04:36.743 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Are you going to make more?"
-Yes.

00:04:36.743 --> 00:04:39.178 size:78% position:39%
-And is it exhausting
that you finally got

00:04:39.178 --> 00:04:41.748 align:start size:94% position:6%
this product you made out,
and immediately

00:04:41.748 --> 00:04:42.448 size:81% position:41% line:92%
people are like, "so?"

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:46.019
-This is what's so helpful
about the experience of Netflix.

00:04:46.019 --> 00:04:47.353 line:92%
And I'm so grateful that I had,

00:04:47.353 --> 00:04:49.656 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, these seven years
we just finished.

00:04:49.656 --> 00:04:51.157 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know,
"Orange Is the New Black"

00:04:51.157 --> 00:04:52.592 align:start size:94% position:6%
is very heavy.
Very heavy scene.

00:04:52.592 --> 00:04:54.661 size:84% position:42%
And -- Yeah, thanks.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:54.661 --> 00:04:57.430 align:start size:94% position:6%
Thanks, thanks.
You know, we're very tight.

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:01.734
So, but what I learned
from that show, thankfully, is,

00:05:01.734 --> 00:05:04.370 size:78% position:39%
you know, all year --
all year -- everybody

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:08.174
waits for the show to come out.
It's a 13-hour show.

00:05:08.174 --> 00:05:10.376 align:start size:94% position:6%
They consume it
in under 30 minutes flat.

00:05:10.376 --> 00:05:11.711 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Light laughter ]
I don't know how they do it.

00:05:11.711 --> 00:05:14.380 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's where I learned about
the space-time continuum

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:16.549 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I use so effectively
in "Russian Doll,"

00:05:16.549 --> 00:05:19.218
was in the way that these people
break time on Netflix.

00:05:19.218 --> 00:05:22.755 size:81% position:41%
And so I'm used to it.
You know what I mean?

00:05:22.755 --> 00:05:25.391 align:start size:94% position:6%
This idea of, like,
"I like it. Give me more."

00:05:25.391 --> 00:05:28.928
It's a compliment, but it's
important for us to remember,

00:05:28.928 --> 00:05:33.032 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know,
like Janis Joplin said,

00:05:33.032 --> 00:05:35.802 align:start size:94% position:6%
"We've only got one day, man."
Et cetera.

00:05:35.802 --> 00:05:39.405 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
So take it easy, take it easy.

00:05:39.405 --> 00:05:40.006 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Applause ]

00:05:40.006 --> 00:05:43.776 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, Natasha, I -- you know,
I texted you

00:05:43.776 --> 00:05:46.813
as soon as I watched the show.
And it is such a special show.

00:05:46.813 --> 00:05:48.181 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And I wrote,
"New phone. Who dis?"

00:05:48.181 --> 00:05:50.416 size:81% position:41%
-You really did write,
"New phone. Who dis?"

00:05:50.416 --> 00:05:53.152 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I did. I knew it was you.
No, I didn't.

00:05:53.152 --> 00:05:56.022 align:start size:94% position:6%
-[ Laughs ]
I really, like, wore my heart.

00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:57.657
I was like, "You've made
this very special thing."

00:05:57.657 --> 00:06:00.560 align:start size:94% position:6%
And you wrote,
"Thank you. Who dis?"

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:02.929 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:06:02.929 --> 00:06:04.430 align:start size:94% position:6%
-But I feel like
you changed your number.

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:07.100
In that moment, I was like,
"Oh, Seth has a different number

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:08.534 align:start size:94% position:6%
for when he likes stuff.
Gotcha."

00:06:08.534 --> 00:06:10.036 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Exactly, yeah.
I'm like, "Ooh,

00:06:10.036 --> 00:06:12.472 align:start size:94% position:6%
I better take out
my compliment phone."

00:06:12.472 --> 00:06:13.039 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:06:13.039 --> 00:06:17.677
But it is -- It is a really --
It is a really special show.

00:06:17.677 --> 00:06:19.679
And I know a lot of great people
worked on it.

00:06:19.679 --> 00:06:23.683 align:start size:94% position:6%
But the heart of it is
what a special person you are.

00:06:23.683 --> 00:06:24.717 line:92%
And it's just so great to see it

00:06:24.717 --> 00:06:26.686 align:start size:94% position:6%
over the course
of a season of television.

00:06:26.686 --> 00:06:28.321 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Seth, thank you.
-And thanks for being here.

00:06:28.321 --> 00:06:29.455 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It's just always
such a pleasure.

00:06:29.455 --> 00:06:31.924 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Thank you.
-Natasha Lyonne, everybody.

